From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] clk: qcom: gcc-ipq5018: keep the NSS core clock enabled

The bootloader leaves gcc_ubi0_core_clk (the clock branch feeding the
UBI32 network subsystem core) enabled. Linux has no consumer for it
until the NSS driver probes some 25 s into boot, so clk_disable_unused()
gates it at late_initcall and the driver re-enables it later.

On the GL.iNet GL-B3000 (IPQ5018) that gate-and-regate leaves the core in
a state from which the Wi-Fi datapath offload (wifili) does not recover:
the first WPA2 client to associate never completes the 4-way handshake,
the firmware's N2H counters freeze and the core has to be power-cycled.
The failure only shows on cold boots, where the core comes out of the
bootloader state; after a warm reboot the driver has already reset the
core once and the same gate is harmless.

Bisection with a per-clock keep list over the 194 GCC clocks isolates
this single branch: keeping it alone across clk_disable_unused() gives
4/4 cold-boot survivals, dropping it from an otherwise identical keep
list kills the core on the first client every time (0/3), and the global
clk_ignore_unused workaround that has been in use so far (20/20) does
nothing more than this for the NSS path. The other NSS-node branches
(cfg, dbg, utcm, nc_axi, snoc_axi) and the Q6/WCSS branches are not
involved.

Mark the branch CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED so it stays in the state the
bootloader left it in until the NSS driver takes it over.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-ipq5018.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-ipq5018.c
@@ -2881,7 +2881,7 @@ static struct clk_branch gcc_ubi0_core_c
 				&ubi0_core_clk_src.clkr.hw,
 			},
 			.num_parents = 1,
-			.flags = CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT,
+			.flags = CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT | CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED,
 			.ops = &clk_branch2_ops,
 		},
 	},
